<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <color name="purple_200">#FFBB86FC</color>
    <color name="purple_700">#FF3700B3</color>
    <color name="teal_200">#FF03DAC5</color>
    <color name="teal_700">#FF018786</color>
    <color name="black">#FF000000</color>
    <color name="main_fragment">#000000</color>
    <color name="white">#FFFFFFFF</color>
    <!-- Brand accent already used throughout the Myata artwork -->
    <color name="myata_accent">#00B7CE</color>

    <!--
      3.6.6 semantic colours - LIGHT.

      The eleven roles approved as v1 in
      tools/figma-export/canonical/semantic-tokens.json, which states it is the
      source of truth for this file. Each was derived by matching equivalent
      nodes across the two canonical Figma pages, and each carries confidence
      CONFIRMED there - except on_primary, which is EVIDENCED_NOT_BOUND: the
      colour is visible on the play/pause glyph but was not bound in Figma
      because the same hexes appear elsewhere for unrelated things.

      ADDITIVE ONLY. Nothing references these yet. The legacy colours above keep
      their names and values, and screens keep resolving them, until each screen
      is migrated in Phase B. Retiring a legacy colour before its last user moves
      would be a silent visual change.

      The dark counterparts live in values-night/colors.xml. There is no
      values-night entry for the legacy colours, so those resolve here in both
      modes exactly as they did before.

      Android TV does not use these. It reads values/colors_tv.xml, which holds
      literals precisely so this file can move without touching TV.
    -->
    <color name="background">#F8F9FA</color>
    <color name="surface">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="surface_container">#F8F9FA</color>
    <color name="navigation_container">#EDEEEF</color>
    <color name="menu_surface">#F8F9FA</color>
    <color name="text_primary">#191C1D</color>
    <color name="text_heading">#003056</color>
    <color name="text_secondary">#42474E</color>
    <color name="outline">#E1E3E4</color>
    <color name="primary">#1C4771</color>
    <color name="on_primary">#FFFFFF</color>

    <!--
      TWO ROLES ADDED BY THE COLLECTION F1/F3 MIGRATION.

      `menu_outline` is the stroke of every menu and bottom-sheet surface. It is
      NOT `outline`: the canonical `Bottom Sheet / Найти трек на стриминге` and
      `Menu / Плеер` both draw #1C3F5F here in light, a navy border, where
      `outline` is the neutral #E1E3E4 - and they do it consistently across both
      nodes, so this is a real role and not a light-page slip. In dark the two
      coincide at #466D8F, which is exactly why the role has to be named: they
      are equal there by coincidence of the palette, not by definition.
      Provenance: canonical light 2444:18768, dark 2444:18657 (frame strokes).

      `error` is the destructive action, and today it has exactly one user: the
      `Удалить из коллекции` row on the per-track sheet. It is graded v2
      PROPOSED / confidence WEAK in tools/figma-export/screens-3.6.6/spec/
      tokens.mjs - the dark value is the 'Token / error' swatch from the dark UI
      KIT, the light value is proposed, and NO canonical screen shows an error
      state. It is recorded here with that grading so the next reader knows the
      light value is the one to re-confirm when a canonical error state exists.
    -->
    <color name="menu_outline">#1C3F5F</color>
    <color name="error">#B3261E</color>

    <!--
      FIXED BRAND COLOURS - not semantic roles.

      These are identical on the canonical Light and Dark pages, deliberately, so
      a light/dark pair cannot express them. They are declared here only, with NO
      values-night counterpart: that absence is what makes "the same in both
      themes" a structural property of the resource rather than a coincidence
      someone can quietly break later.

      Do not fold these into the eleven semantic roles, and do not add night
      variants without a change to the frozen design.

      Provenance: canonical HOME, BottomNavBar - light node 2393:1629,
      dark node 2444:10351. Both pages carry the same values.
    -->
    <!-- active nav pill, BottomNavBar > Background (79x46, r=9999) -->
    <color name="brand_nav_active_container">#FFCCFF</color>
    <!-- active nav icon and label, that node's Icon and Margin > label -->
    <color name="brand_nav_active_content">#00723D</color>

    <!--
      Donation CTA in "О нас". Same category as the nav pill above: the frozen
      ABOUT US page carries these identical values on the light and the dark
      page, so they are fixed brand colours and get no night variant.

      on_primary cannot carry the label: it is #FFFFFF in Light but #0F253E in
      Dark, and the frozen button is white in both.

      Provenance: canonical ABOUT US, Section 1 "Помочь проекту" > Button
      (239x52, r=12) - light node 2411:31659, dark page identical.
    -->
    <color name="brand_donate_cta_container">#003056</color>
    <color name="brand_donate_cta_content">#FFFFFF</color>

    <!--
      Mini Player (B2), frozen 3.6.6 canonical.

      Provenance: `Now Playing Mini Player (Floating above Bottom Nav)` on HOME,
      COLLECTION, COLLECTION pusto and ABOUT US of both canonical pages. Where a
      screen carries two stacked copies of the pill, the values below are the
      topmost one - the copy that actually renders. The lower copies are earlier
      residue (title/artist/icon all #8FB6E6) and are ignored.

      Four of the five are FIXED BRAND COLOURS, in the sense the block above
      defines: the Light and the Dark page carry the same value, so there is no
      night variant and the absence of one is what keeps them identical. The pill
      reads as `primary` on the Light page (#1C4771 is the light primary) and as
      `surface_container` on the Dark page (#1C4771 is the dark surfaceContainer),
      which is why one hex is legible in both themes and why neither semantic role
      can express it on its own.

      Only the artist line genuinely differs between the pages, so it is the one
      that gets a values-night counterpart.
    -->
    <!-- pill fill, r=16 -->
    <color name="brand_mini_player_container">#1C4771</color>
    <!-- 1px INSIDE stroke, #FFFFFF at 10% -->
    <color name="brand_mini_player_hairline">#1AFFFFFF</color>
    <!-- track title -->
    <color name="brand_mini_player_title">#E0E8F2</color>
    <!-- play / pause glyph -->
    <color name="brand_mini_player_icon">#F5F7FA</color>

    <!--
      Artist line. #DCEBFE on all four Light instances; #B3C4D1 on HOME and
      COLLECTION in Dark, with ABOUT US the one outlier at #DCEBFE @ 80%. The Dark
      UI KIT component `Component / Dark / Mini-player / *` settles it at #B3C4D1,
      which is also exactly text_secondary in Dark - but not in Light, where
      text_secondary is #42474E and would vanish on the navy pill. Hence its own
      pair rather than a reference to the role.
    -->
    <color name="mini_player_artist">#DCEBFE</color>

    <!--
      PLAYER header label - "Сейчас играет" and the reserved trailing slot.

      Provenance: canonical PLAYER, `Player Section > Mobile Header (Subtle)`.
      #42474E light, #F5F7FA dark.

      It gets its own pair because it lands on a different role in each theme:
      #F5F7FA is exactly text_primary in dark, #42474E is exactly text_secondary in
      light. Binding it to either role would be right in one theme and wrong in the
      other. Compare the track artist just below, which is text_secondary in dark
      and only a near neighbour of it in light - one role, so one token.
    -->
    <color name="player_header_label">#42474E</color>

    <!--
      PLAYER swipe indicator, the two inactive pages.

      Provenance: canonical PLAYER, `Player Section > swipe`. The active marker is
      `primary` at full opacity in both themes - #1C4771 light, #5FD9B4 dark - and
      needs no resource of its own. The inactive ones do, because the two pages
      express them differently: light draws `primary` at 50% opacity, dark a solid
      #6F899F.

      So this is a pair, and the light half keeps the frozen expression rather
      than the composited result: #801C4771 is what the design says, and over the
      #F8F9FA background it lands on the #8AA0B6 it is meant to look like.
    -->
    <color name="player_swipe_inactive">#801C4771</color>

    <!--
      PLAYER `like` glyph, at rest.

      Provenance: canonical PLAYER, `Controls > like > Container > Icon`. #3F4A3C
      light, #F5F7FA dark. (Read off the *visible* `Controls` row - each frozen
      page carries a second, hidden copy of that row with stale values in it.)

      Same shape of pair as player_header_label: #F5F7FA is exactly text_primary
      in dark, and the light value is a near neighbour of text_secondary - the
      neighbouring `dislike` glyph is exactly #42474E, and this one carries a
      green cast of the same weight. So the light value is kept verbatim rather
      than bound to a role, which is what the frozen frame draws.

      Not text_primary, which is what the app used for both action slots: #191C1D
      is markedly darker than either frozen glyph in light.
    -->
    <color name="player_like">#3F4A3C</color>

    <!--
      PLAYER control row, the trailing action.

      Provenance: canonical PLAYER, `Controls > dislike > Container > Icon`.
      #42474E light, #F5F7FA dark, read off the visible `Controls` row.

      The frozen row defines that slot unambiguously and identically on both
      pages, so the History entry that occupies it until its own phase takes the
      colour even though its glyph and its behaviour are still the app's. It was
      text_primary, which is markedly darker in light than either frozen glyph and
      read as a mismatch beside `like`.

      A separate resource from player_header_label and collection_header_action
      rather than a reuse of them: the three carry the same values today by
      coincidence of the palette, not because one is defined as another.
    -->
    <color name="player_control_action">#42474E</color>

    <!--
      COLLECTION header trailing action - the frozen 3-dot overflow glyph.

      Provenance: canonical `COLLECTION` / `COLLECTION_dark`, `Header - TopAppBar
      > Button:margin > Button > Container > Icon` (4x16). #42474E light,
      #F5F7FA dark.

      It needs its own pair for the same reason player_header_label does: #42474E
      is exactly text_secondary in light while #F5F7FA is exactly text_primary in
      dark, so no single role is right in both themes. It is deliberately a
      separate resource from player_header_label rather than a reuse of it - the
      two carry the same values today by coincidence of the palette, not because
      one screen's header action is defined as the other's.
    -->
    <color name="collection_header_action">#42474E</color>

    <!--
      COLLECTION empty-state illustration.

      Provenance: canonical `COLLECTION pusto` / `COLLECTION pusto_dark`,
      `Collection List > Track Item 1 > Circle` and `> Frame 5`.

      ILLUSTRATION-SPECIFIC, NOT SEMANTIC ROLES. These are the colours of one
      drawing and they are deliberately kept out of the token system: #6750A4 and
      #EADDFF are Material 3 baseline values, #E74C3C and #FFD8E4 are the heart,
      and none of them describes a surface, a text or an outline anywhere else in
      the app. Do not fold them into the eleven roles and do not reuse them.

      Four of the six are identical on both canonical pages and so carry no night
      variant, by the same contract the fixed brand colours above use. The two
      disc rings are the only ones the design changes between themes.
    -->
    <color name="collection_empty_disc_outer">#F3EDF7</color>
    <color name="collection_empty_disc_inner">#EADDFF</color>
    <color name="collection_empty_disc_hub">#6750A4</color>
    <color name="collection_empty_disc_spindle">#FFFFFF</color>
    <color name="collection_empty_badge_plate">#FFD8E4</color>
    <color name="collection_empty_badge_heart">#E74C3C</color>

    <!--
      HOME playlist card, behind the cover art.

      Provenance: canonical HOME, `Mint Playlists Section > Horizontal Scroll
      Container > Playlist N > Background` - light node 2393:1650's section, dark
      2444:10372's. Both pages carry #EDEEEF, so it belongs with the fixed brand
      colours above and gets no night variant.

      It is only ever visible while the artwork loads: the image fill is CROP and
      covers the card completely once it arrives.
    -->
    <color name="brand_playlist_card_placeholder">#EDEEEF</color>

    <!--
      PLAYER album art backdrop.

      Provenance: canonical PLAYER, `Player Section > Album Art (Organic Shape) >
      Background+Blur`, 229x229 r20. #1C4771 on both pages, so it is fixed and gets
      no night variant.

      In Figma it is a 229 square sitting behind an opaque 239 artwork, so all but
      one pixel of it is covered and it never really shows. Here it is the artwork
      card's own background, which is the one state where it earns its place: the
      colour the frozen design put behind the art is what fills the card while the
      art loads, instead of a hole.
    -->
    <color name="brand_player_artwork_backdrop">#1C4771</color>

    <!--
      PLAYER Broadcast History, "Показать ещё" (Phase C).

      Provenance: canonical `Broadcast History Section > Button:margin > Button` -
      light node 2396:30867, dark 2444:18353.

      Both of these are PAIRS, not roles, and neither could be folded into an
      existing token without being wrong in one theme:

        container  #F8F9FA / #1C3F5F   light is exactly background and
                                       surface_container; dark is neither -
                                       surface_container is #1C4771 there, four
                                       steps away, and surface is #142D47.

        label      #003056 / #5FD9B4   light is exactly text_heading; dark is
                                       exactly primary. text_heading is #F5F7FA
                                       in dark and primary is #1C4771 in light,
                                       so each role is right in one theme and
                                       wrong in the other.

      The button's stroke is not here: it is #E1E3E4 / #466D8F, which is the
      `outline` role on both pages exactly, so it takes the token.
    -->
    <color name="player_history_more_container">#F8F9FA</color>
    <color name="player_history_more_label">#003056</color>

    <!--
      PLAYER Broadcast History, the 40x40 r6 behind a row's album art.

      Provenance: `History Item N > Container > Background` - light node
      2396:30798, dark 2444:18302. #E1E3E4 on both canonical pages, so it belongs
      with the fixed brand colours above and gets NO night variant.

      It reads as `outline` in light only by coincidence of the palette - dark
      `outline` is #466D8F while this stays #E1E3E4 - which is exactly why it is a
      literal here and not that token. Same job as
      brand_playlist_card_placeholder: the frozen `Album Art` child carries an
      IMAGE fill set to CROP, so this is what shows while the cover loads, and it
      is all that shows when ArtworkRepository finds nothing.
    -->
    <color name="brand_player_history_artwork_placeholder">#E1E3E4</color>

    <!--
      PLAYER play/pause glyph.

      Provenance: the owner-supplied FINAL assets in
      tools/figma-export/player-icons/owner-final - the `stroke` on the paths in
      play_light.svg / pause_light.svg and their dark counterparts.

      A PAIR rather than a role, and specifically NOT `on_primary`. Dark's
      #0F253E is exactly `on_primary` there, but light's #F8F9FA is not - that
      role is #FFFFFF - and `on_primary` is bound to `colorOnPrimary` in
      Theme.Myata.Base, so moving it to satisfy this glyph would repaint every
      Material component in the app for a three-step change on one control.

      The surface under it needs nothing: `primary` already resolves to the
      #1C4771 and #5FD9B4 the same files fill their 80x80 rect with.
    -->
    <color name="player_play_glyph">#F8F9FA</color>

    <!--
      ABOUT US (Phase B), frozen 3.6.6 canonical.

      Provenance: `ABOUT US` 2411:31594 on CURRENT ANDROID UI - LIGHT and
      `ABOUT US_dark` 2444:18567 on CURRENT ANDROID UI - DARK.

      Most of the screen needed nothing new. The header title, the "О нас" and
      "Ещё Радио Мята" headings and the "Читать подробнее" label are
      `text_heading` on both pages exactly; the paragraphs are `text_secondary`
      exactly; every card and button stroke on Section 2 is `outline` exactly;
      and the social tiles land on `surface_container` in BOTH themes (#F8F9FA /
      #1C4771), which is the cleanest role match on the screen. The donate button
      already had brand_donate_cta_* from the earlier CTA migration and reuses
      them unchanged.

      What follows is only what those roles cannot express.
    -->

    <!--
      The two donation cards are FIXED BRAND COLOURS in the sense the nav pill
      block above defines: the light and the dark page carry identical values, so
      a light/dark pair cannot express them and the ABSENCE of a values-night
      entry is what keeps them identical.

      The mint reads as `primary` on the Dark page (#5FD9B4 is the dark primary)
      and as nothing at all on the Light page; the navy is the mirror case. That
      is the same trap brand_mini_player_container documents - one hex legible in
      both themes, and neither semantic role able to carry it alone.

      Provenance: `Boosty Subscription Card` 2411:31635 / 2444:18582 and
      `One-time Donation Card` 2411:31649 / 2444:18594.
    -->
    <color name="brand_about_boosty_container">#5FD9B4</color>
    <color name="brand_about_onetime_container">#1C4771</color>
    <!-- Boosty card heading and body, #1C4771 on both pages -->
    <color name="brand_about_boosty_content">#1C4771</color>
    <!-- Boosty card "Подписаться" button, 2411:31645 / 2444:18592 -->
    <color name="brand_about_boosty_button_container">#1C4771</color>
    <!-- Social tile 1px stroke, #1C3F5F on both pages - 2411:31678 / 2444:18624 -->
    <color name="brand_about_social_outline">#1C3F5F</color>

    <!--
      The genuine pairs. Each of these lands on a different role in each theme,
      or on no role at all, which is what forces a values-night counterpart
      rather than a token reference.
    -->

    <!--
      Section 2 card fill. #142D47 is exactly `surface` in dark, but light
      `surface` is #FFFFFF against this card's #F3F4F5 - a visible step on a
      #F8F9FA background - so the role is right in one theme only.
      Provenance: `Section 2: About Us` 2411:31663 / 2444:18606.
    -->
    <color name="about_section_container">#F3F4F5</color>

    <!--
      "Читать подробнее" fill. Same shape of problem, opposite corner: #142D47 is
      `surface` in dark while light draws #F8F9FA, which is `background` and
      `surface_container` but not `surface`. Its stroke and label are `outline`
      and `text_heading` and take those tokens.
      Provenance: `Button` 2424:758 / 2444:18618.
    -->
    <color name="about_read_more_container">#F8F9FA</color>

    <!--
      One-time card heading and body. #F5F7FA is exactly `text_primary` in dark;
      light draws #FFFFFF on the navy card, where `text_primary` (#191C1D) would
      be unreadable.
      Provenance: 2411:31655 / 2444:18600 and 2411:31657 / 2444:18602.
    -->
    <color name="about_onetime_content">#FFFFFF</color>

    <!--
      The two card chips. Their fills, strokes and labels all invert between the
      pages and none of them tracks a role in both.

      The one deliberate normalisation on this screen: the light one-time chip
      stroke is #452149 in Figma - a plum that appears nowhere else in the
      design and reads as residue from an earlier palette beside its own
      #1C4771 label. It is kept verbatim rather than "corrected", because
      Figma wins on any disagreement and a drive-by colour change is exactly
      what the parity validator exists to catch.

      Provenance: `Background+Border` 2411:31638 / 2444:18585 (Boosty) and
      2411:31652 / 2444:18597 (one-time).
    -->
    <color name="about_boosty_chip_outline">#006D3A</color>
    <color name="about_boosty_chip_label">#003056</color>
    <color name="about_onetime_chip_container">#F8F9FA</color>
    <color name="about_onetime_chip_outline">#452149</color>
    <color name="about_onetime_chip_label">#1C4771</color>

</resources>
